Not a bad synopsis Janis. However, then properly played the racket is an
effective strategy. The problem is that most folks don't play it
properly. Some nuances:

1) It's not necessary to keep your XL at level 1. The racket plays just
at well at higher XLs. However, the lower you can keep your XL, the less
likely that you'll run into a monster you can't handle. I usually end up
in minetown at XL2 or XL3.

2) Armor is critical. Even if your character is damaged by wearing armor
(mostly spellcasters) wear all non cursed armor during the run.

3) As Janis pointed out, the pet is crucial. You'll want to spend time
making sure that it grows up large and that you stay nearby. 

4) Hit points are critical too. There are several techniques for gaining HPs
without gaining XLs. Most times I boost my HPs into the mid 20s even
though the character is at XL2.

For anyone who wants to practice, the best character is a Gnomish
healer. As a gnome you'll have an easier time in the Mines. As a healer
you'll be able to track your pet's level and HP, heal yourself and the
pet, and to boost your HPs without gaining experience.

Once you get the hang of it, you can apply the same techniques to other
characters.

Like I said when done properly it's not as risky as it seems. It teaches
patience, using your pet, and gives a specific early plan in the early
game. Also sice you clear out every shop that you find, you end up with
both a ton of useful stuff and a ton of useful information about stuff.

